## Why This Is Asked

This tests ability to design end-to-end, multi-tenant privacy-conscious tests for streaming data, including isolation, schema evolution, and canary risk.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy isolation
- Data privacy and retention
- Schema evolution and backward compatibility
- Streaming backpressure and performance
- Canary rollout and rollback criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: simple data leakage check
function checkIsolation(records) {
  const seenTenants = new Set();
  for (const r of records) {
    if (seenTenants.has(r.tenantId)) return false;
    seenTenants.add(r.tenantId);
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generate tenant-scoped synthetic data with privacy constraints?
- What instrumentation would you add to verify no cross-tenant leakage under burst load?

## Why This Is Asked

To evaluate how you design practical, scalable test plans for orchestrators with dependencies, timeouts, retries, and cross-region considerations; ensures coverage beyond single-service testing.

## Key Concepts

- DAG scheduling and dependency resolution
- Timeout handling and backoff/retry policies
- Idempotence and replay safety
- Failure propagation and rollback semantics
- End-to-end vs component tests; observability and metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Jest-like test skeleton for a 2-task DAG
test('DAG A->B completes end-to-end with retry', async () => {
  const dag = new DagRunner(mockTasks);
  dag.run('A'); // A depends on nothing
  // mock A to fail first, then succeed, verify B runs after A completes
  expect(dag.completed).toBe(true);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate regional failure and ensure idempotent replays across regions?
- What metrics would you collect to validate SLAs during high load or canary rollouts?
